module async/scope v1.0.0;

/*
=head1 NAME

async/scope - structured lifetime for language futures

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<FutureGroup<T>> owns the completion lifecycle of registered C<Future<T>>
values. C<start()> registers and eagerly starts a future without taking it away
from the caller. Registering the same future identity again is idempotent.

    let group is FutureGroup<int> = FutureGroup();
    let first = Worker(id: 1).run();
    let second = Worker(id: 2).run();

    group.start(first);
    group.start(second);
    await group.wait();

C<wait()> seals the group, waits for every child and observes all failures.
C<cancel()> also seals it and requests cancellation of unfinished children;
call C<wait()> afterwards when using a group directly. Explicit cancellation
accepts cancellation-only child outcomes, including children already settled
by a prior await cancellation; real secondary cleanup failures remain reported.
Without explicit group cancellation, a cancelled child remains a failure.
Registration after
sealing throws C<FinishedFutureGroup>.

C<active()> counts children whose success, failure or cancellation cleanup has
not settled yet. It is an observation for supervision and diagnostics, not a
polling substitute for C<wait()>.

Independent child failures are reported in registration order. The first is
the primary failure and later failures remain ordered secondary diagnostics.
Typed catches continue to see the unchanged primary error.

C<FutureScope<T,R>> binds that group lifecycle to one async body. The body is
invoked once, receives only the C<Supervising<T>> capability and may register
children without gaining a separate way to finish the group. The scope waits
for every child before publishing the body result:

    object Job {
        Job();
        async run() is int { 21; }
    }

    object Batch {
        Batch();

        async run(group is Supervising<int>) is int {
            group.start(Job().run());
            42;
        }
    }

    let scope is FutureScope<int, int> = FutureScope(action: Batch().run);
    let answer = await scope.result();

If the body fails, or the caller cancels C<scope.result()>, unfinished children
are cancelled and their cleanup is awaited. The body failure remains primary;
independent child cleanup failures are retained as ordered secondary failures.

One scope owns one homogeneous child result type. Nest another scope when one
operation owns children of another type.

=cut
*/

role Supervising<T> {
    start(origin is Future<T>) is void;
    active() is int;
    cancel() is void;
}

object FutureGroup<T> does Supervising<T> {
    FutureGroup() {}

    start(origin is Future<T>) is void {
        throw FinishedFutureGroup();
    }

    active() is int {
        panic "FutureGroup.active is a VM intrinsic";
    }

    cancel() is void {
        panic "FutureGroup.cancel is a VM intrinsic";
    }

    async wait() is FutureGroup<T> {
        panic "FutureGroup.wait is a VM intrinsic";
        self;
    }
}

object FutureScope<T, R> {
    has action is async (Supervising<T>) is R;

    FutureScope(:action);

    async result() is R {
        let group is FutureGroup<T> = FutureGroup();
        await group.wait();
        panic "FutureScope.result is a VM intrinsic";
    }
}

object FinishedFutureGroup does Error {
    FinishedFutureGroup();

    text() is string {
        "future group is already finishing";
    }
}
